    {
      "id": "techcrunch-2026-06-16-snap-finally-debuts-its-long-awaited-ar-glasses-specs-and-oo",
      "title": "Snap finally debuts its long-awaited AR glasses, Specs, and, oof, they aren’t cheap",
      "summary": "For over a decade now, Snap has been working on this device. Now the glasses are finally here. So what stands out on first impression?",
      "body": "At a spatial AI convention in Long Beach on Tuesday, Snap finally unveiled Specs, its long-awaited consumer smart glasses, and at $2,195, they don’t come cheap.\nSpecs will be available for preorder on June 16, with a $200 refundable deposit, and are expected to ship this fall in the U.S., the U.K., and France. The price is well above most Meta Ray-Bans (which can run as low as $350), though still far below the Apple Vision Pro’s $3,500 starting price. Either way, it’s steep enough to put Specs out of reach for most everyday consumers.\nFor over a decade now, Snap has been working on this device. Despite this, the last time the company released a consumer-facing version of the glasses was in 2019 — its latest iterations have been developer only. Earlier this year, Snap spun off a new company to focus exclusively on bringing the product to market.\nNow the glasses are finally here. So what stands out on first impression?\nVisually, Specs looks like a fairly normal pair of glasses — albeit a slightly bulkier, goggle-like pair. That extra bulk comes down to a key design choice: Unlike some competitors, all of the computing takes place on the actual device, and it conveniently lacks a puck or tether.\nSpecs runs on two Snapdragon processors, and it comes with up to four hours of continuous battery life, plus a charging case that extends that to 20 hours total.\nBut what can you actually do with them? For starters, there are games, including ones that support shared multiplayer sessions between two users. Snap calls this feature “EyeConnect,” and it’s activated simply by two wearers making eye contact with each other.\nYou can also watch videos (Snap says the display offers a 51-degree field of view and 16 million colors), record point-of-view footage, and, at least in theory, get work done since the glasses allow you to surf the internet, connect to productivity apps, and check your email.\nOne standout feature is contextual AI. Look at an object and ask about it, and the glasses can pull up information on what you’re seeing — a glimpse of the kind of AI-assistant layer that’s becoming a competitive battleground in this category.\nThe glasses come in two sizes — a 47 mm model, which weighs 132 grams (approximately 4.6 ounces) and a 52 mm model, which weighs 136 grams (4.7 ounces). That makes them noticeably heavier than Meta’s Ray-Bans — the first-gen Wayfarers weigh under an ounce — but far lighter than Apple’s Vision Pro, which tips the scales at 26.4 to 28.2 ounces.\nThere are also privacy protections. On privacy, Specs follows Meta’s lead with a built-in LED light that glows while the device is recording. The company says that users will also have control over what data is stored, synced, or deleted.\nWhen I was in Las Vegas for CES earlier this year, the Snap team let me demo an earlier version of the glasses. It was fun to play around with the apps, and I was impressed by the contextual AI, but the device was also quite heavy and, after running for a while, it could get hot.\nFrom the looks of it, Snap has since slimmed down the hardware, making the glasses both less obtrusive and more efficient.\nThe bigger question is whether this decade-long innovation marathon will result in some sort of viable business for Snap. Specs enters a market that is increasingly saturated with competitors. Meta now leads with its popular Ray-Ban series, and Google has announced its own new line of AI-powered glasses.\nAs for who these glasses are for, Snap says it’s aiming first at tech enthusiasts, developers, and studios — though at $2,200, that audience is going to need some deep pockets to match their enthusiasm.\nThe cumbersome price highlights an ongoing dilemma for the smart glasses industry — which is that consumer interest has yet to go beyond mere curiosity to the kind of enthusiasm that can result in consistent profits.\nAs a result, no one is really making a profit. Even the industry’s current champ — Meta — is losing tons of money on its AR-development division.\nSnap has struggled over the past few years with a wobbling stock and a recent decline in North American user engagement. Despite launching well over a decade ago, the company is still not consistently profitable. In April, it also underwent a round of layoffs. Will Specs be the product to turn it all around and usher in the next evolution in computing? I guess we’ll have to wait and see.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nLucas is a senior writer at TechCrunch, where he covers artificial intelligence, consumer tech, and startups. He previously covered AI and cybersecurity at Gizmodo.\nYou can contact Lucas by emailing lucas.ropek@techcrunch.com.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Lucas Ropek",
      "publishedAt": "2026-06-16T17:00:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/SPECS27_52_Front_View81.png?resize=1200,675",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/16/snap-finally-debuts-its-long-awaited-ar-glasses-specs-and-oof-they-arent-cheap/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-06-16-qualcomm-s-latest-chip-hints-that-more-powerful-smart-glasse",
      "title": "Qualcomm’s latest chip hints that more powerful smart glasses could be on the way",
      "summary": "Smart glasses are still a nascent category, but chipmaker Qualcomm is hard at work upgrading the silicon to power the next wave of XR devices: the Snapdragon Reality Elite.\nAlthough Qualcomm is announcing the chip today at Augmented World Expo, we've technically already gotten a hands-on with a device powered by the new chip at last month's Google I/O: the forthcoming Aura glasses for Android XR. ",
      "body": "Smart glasses are still a nascent category, but chipmaker Qualcomm is hard at work upgrading the silicon to power the next wave of XR devices: the Snapdragon Reality Elite.\nAlthough Qualcomm is announcing the chip today at Augmented World Expo, we’ve technically already gotten a hands-on with a device powered by the new chip at last month’s Google I/O: the forthcoming Aura glasses for Android XR. At the time, Xreal and Google were coy about the processor upgrades to the long-awaited spectacles. Turns out, it was the Reality Elite.\nSpec-wise, the new chip focuses on across-the-board performance upgrades. The GPU gets a 60 percent bump, the CPU gets a 30 percent increase, and the NPU gets “up to 160 percent higher performance.” It supports 4.4K resolution at 90 frames per second per eye and less latency. Battery life has also been improved by up to 20 percent, and Qualcomm was able to improve cooling as well by boosting power efficiency. Supposedly, while handling heavy workloads, the Reality Elite will remain up to 12 degrees Celsius cooler than Qualcomm’s last-gen XR chips.\nIn other words, this chip ought to support better visuals for immersive XR experiences, more power to handle larger LLMs for AI features, and lighter, longer-lasting glasses. You know, all the technical problems currently plaguing the smart glasses space.\nThis — plus the Snapdragon Wear Elite chip that Qualcomm introduced back at Mobile World Congress in February — offers a few important clues about what we’re likely to see from wearable devices this fall and in 2027. (After all, as a components maker, Qualcomm is creating chips to meet the specific demands of partners like Meta and Google.) Both the Wear Elite and Reality Elite can be used to power smart glasses. The former is likely to be found in audio-only glasses, while the latter will likely be used for power-hungry display glasses with AI-centric features. Either way, the fact that Qualcomm boosted AI performance across both chips indicates gadget makers are gung-ho on stuffing more AI into glasses, smartwatches, fitness trackers, pins, and pendants. The battery and cooling improvements are also a tacit acknowledgement that many smart glasses with displays currently struggle with the tradeoffs between bulky or unwieldy designs and all-day battery life. The risk of overheating has also been a major problem for smart glasses makers when it comes to offering more advanced features. (Because no one wants a pair of glasses to burn their faces.) Provided the Snapdragon Reality Elite’s upgrades can deliver genuine improvements in this area, it might not be too long before we start seeing some more impressive AI wearables hit the market.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "AI",
        "Gadgets",
        "News",
        "Tech",
        "Wearable"
      ],
      "author": "Victoria Song",
      "publishedAt": "2026-06-16T17:00:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/06/snapdragonreality.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,3.4613147178592,100,93.077370564282",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/gadgets/950229/qualcomm-snapdragon-reality-elite-xr-smart-glasses-wearables",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-06-16-the-google-xreal-aura-xr-glasses-are-now-available-to-preord",
      "title": "The Google / Xreal Aura XR glasses are now available to preorder",
      "summary": "There’s still no word on the final launch price, however. | Image by Xreal\t\n\nThe Project Aura glasses collaboration between Xreal and Google is now one step closer to being something you can buy. Reservations for the second Android XR device, now dubbed the Xreal Aura, are available for $99 starting today, with a full launch in the US, UK, Japan, Canada, and South Korea expected sometime this Fall",
      "body": "The Project Aura glasses collaboration between Xreal and Google is now one step closer to being something you can buy. Reservations for the second Android XR device, now dubbed the Xreal Aura, are available for $99 starting today, with a full launch in the US, UK, Japan, Canada, and South Korea expected sometime this Fall.\nThe Xreal Aura follows Google’s first Android-powered XR device, the Samsung Galaxy XR headset, which launched at $1,799 in October 2025. We still don’t have a full price for the Aura — which Google has previously described as “a headset masquerading as glasses” — but Best Buy will be the first in-store retail partner when it does become available. Xreal also says that those who reserve within a two-week launch offer will receive $199 credit towards the launch purchase later this year, “saving you $100 on the final price.”\nAs part of this deposit scheme announcement, we know also know that the Xreal Aura weighs under 95 grams, and is powered by the new Snapdragon Reality Elite chip, Qualcomm’s latest XR platform. The chip, in combination with Google’s Android XR, aims to help the Aura glasses to meet demands for “better immersive experiences, higher performance, greater intelligence, and improved power optimization,” according to a statement from Qualcomm’s XR head, Ziad Asghar.\nWe might get some new details about the upcoming launch after Xreal showcases the Aura glasses at Augmented World Expo, which is running between June 15th-18th in Long Beach, California.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Android",
        "Gadgets",
        "Google",
        "News",
        "Tech",
        "Virtual Reality"
      ],
