      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.ycombinator.com/companies/dalus/jobs/5IDmKJt-senior-software-frontend-engineer-germany-office\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48537556\nPoints: 0\n# Comments: 0",
      "body": "AI-Powered Hardware System Design SoftwareSenior Software/Frontend Engineer @ Germany Office€70K - €90K EUR•0.20% - 1.00%•San Francisco, CA, US / Los Angeles, CA, US / Melbourne, VIC, AU / Munich, BY, DE / Remote (DE)RoleEngineering, FrontendVisaUS citizenship/visa not requiredConnect directly with founders of the best YC-funded startups.Apply to role ›About the roleDalus is building a modern, AI-powered platform for systems engineers, the people who design rockets, satellites, defense systems, and EVs. We're replacing the clunky desktop tools the industry has been stuck on for decades.\nWe're hiring a senior software engineer with a focus on Frontend. You'll own the product's interface end to end and set the bar for how it looks and feels. The core challenge: taking dense, complex engineering data and making it feel obvious to use.\nMid to senior, min 5-8 years of experience\nExcellent UI/UX taste and strong React skills\nHave built polished interfaces for real users\nComfortable owning frontend on a small team with a lot of autonomy\nBased in Germany or wider DACH; Munich preferable. German is a plus.\nNice to have: experience with engineering, technical, or developer-facing tools.\nThis is an early, high-ownership role. You'll work directly with the founders and shape the product from the ground up.\nAbout DalusAt Dalus, we're building AI-powered software to reimagine how complex hardware systems are designed — think rockets, satellites, EVs, aircraft, nuclear plants.\nRight now, companies building these systems are held back by legacy tools that were never built for today’s fast-paced engineering teams. We’re changing that. Our platform makes hardware development faster, safer, and more scalable, even with smaller teams.\nWe believe that designing atoms should feel more like working with bits — and that the next industrial renaissance needs better software.",
