      "id": "hackernews-2026-06-14-caddy-compatibility-for-zeroserve-3x-throughput-and-70-lower",
      "title": "Caddy compatibility for zeroserve: 3x throughput and 70% lower latency",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://su3.io/posts/zeroserve-caddy-compat\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48527145\nPoints: 177\n# Comments: 52",
      "body": "zeroserve is a high-performance HTTPS server that runs eBPF scripts in userspace (intro). Now it's got a Caddy-compat mode - when provided a Caddyfile, zeroserve JIT-compiles it to eBPF and then to native x86_64/ARM64 machine code, and runs it in an io_uringevent loop.\nHTTPS reverse proxy, 2 threads, AMD Ryzen 7 3700X. Check CI for original run result.\nTry it with your Caddyfile:\ncurl -fL -o zeroserve https://github.com/losfair/zeroserve/releases/download/v0.2.11/zeroserve-$(uname -m)-linux\nzeroserve runs turing-complete eBPF and you can call custom code from your Caddyfile. For example, to\nreverse-proxy a path to an S3-compatible bucket with AWS SigV4 auth, grab io.su3.aws-sigv4.c and then:\n# zeroserve --plugin io.su3.aws-sigv4.c --caddy Caddyfile\n# Call the `sign_request` method in the eBPF middleware `io.su3.aws-sigv4.o`\n© 2022-2026 Heyang Zhou ·  RSS",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "losfair",
      "publishedAt": "2026-06-14T13:43:08.000Z",
      "imageUrl": null,
      "originalUrl": "https://su3.io/posts/zeroserve-caddy-compat",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
